---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import PdfToJpg from '../islands/PdfToJpg';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Which DPI should I choose?', a: '<strong>150 DPI</strong> is right for screens, messages and most uploads. Choose <strong>300 DPI</strong> for printing or zooming into small text. <strong>72 DPI</strong> gives the smallest files.' },
  { q: 'Can I convert only some pages?', a: 'Yes. Type pages or ranges such as <code>1-3, 5</code> in the Pages box. Leave it empty to convert every page.' },
  { q: 'How do I get all the images at once?', a: 'When there’s more than one page, PDFSherpa bundles the JPGs into a single .zip. You can also save pages one by one from the list.' },
  { q: 'Are images inside the PDF extracted at original quality?', a: 'PDFSherpa renders each page as you see it, including text and graphics, rather than pulling out embedded photos. For the sharpest results, use 300 DPI and Maximum quality.' },
  { q: 'Is my PDF uploaded?', a: 'No. Pages are rendered by pdf.js inside your browser. The PDF never leaves your device.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="pdf-to-jpg"
  title="PDF to JPG Converter – High Quality, Free | PDFSherpa"
  description="Convert PDF pages to JPG images at 72, 150 or 300 DPI. Pick pages, choose quality, and download one image or a zip. No upload, no signup."
  h1="PDF to JPG"
  lead="Turn PDF pages into JPG images at the resolution you need. Convert every page or just a few, then download one image or a zip."
  howToTitle="How to Convert PDF to JPG"
  steps={['Select or drop your PDF.', 'Choose resolution (DPI), quality and, optionally, which pages.', 'Click <strong>Convert to JPG</strong> and download the image or .zip.']}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['jpg-to-pdf', 'compress-image', 'split-pdf', 'compress-pdf']}
>
  <PdfToJpg client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>What PDF to JPG does</h2>
    <p>
      This tool renders each page of your PDF as an image, just as it looks on screen, and saves it as a JPG. Use it when a website accepts
      images but not PDFs, to post a page on social media, or to put a page into a presentation.
    </p>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Open your PDF.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Pick a resolution.</strong> DPI means dots per inch. An A4 page at 150 DPI becomes about 1240 × 1754 pixels, and at 300 DPI about 2480 × 3508.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick a quality.</strong> High is a good balance. Maximum keeps fine detail at a larger file size.</li>
      <li><strong>Optionally choose pages</strong> with ranges like <code>2-4, 7</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Convert and download.</strong> Files are named <code>yourfile-page-1.jpg</code>, <code>yourfile-page-2.jpg</code> and so on.</li>
    </ol>

    <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="tool-article" />

    <h2>Examples</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Upload page 1 of a certificate to a portal that only accepts JPG: Pages <code>1</code>, 150 DPI, High.</li>
      <li>Share slides from a PDF deck in a chat app: all pages, 150 DPI, then save the ones you want from the list.</li>
      <li>Need the image under a size limit? Run it through <a href="/compress-image">Compress Image</a> afterwards.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      300 DPI on very large pages (posters, engineering drawings) needs a lot of memory. PDFSherpa caps the canvas size to stay stable on phones,
      which may lower the effective resolution on huge pages. Password-protected PDFs must be unlocked first.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing the right settings</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Text documents</strong> (contracts, forms): 150 DPI and High quality give crisp, readable images of a few hundred KB each.</li>
      <li><strong>Photos and brochures:</strong> 150–300 DPI with High or Maximum quality to keep colour gradients smooth.</li>
      <li><strong>Upload portals with size limits:</strong> start at 150 DPI, then use <a href="/compress-image">Compress Image</a> to hit an exact KB limit.</li>
      <li><strong>Printing:</strong> 300 DPI and Maximum quality.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each image keeps the page’s proportions, so landscape pages produce landscape images.</p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Conversion runs in your browser with Mozilla’s open-source pdf.js engine. Your document isn’t uploaded or stored anywhere.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
